Here's a concise summary of the implemented simple collaborative filtering (CF) approach using the MovieLens dataset, based on the provided code and experiment results.\
\
What was implemented\
- Model type: Item-based collaborative filtering with adjusted cosine similarities.\
- Data handling:\
  - Tries to load the MovieLens data via LensKit. If that fails, falls back to a synthetic dataset with 943 users, 1682 items, and 100k ratings to keep the pipeline runnable.\
  - Ratings are mapped to dense indices (u for user, i for item) and split into training (80%) and validation (20%) deterministically (seed 42).\
- Baseline statistics:\
  - Global mean rating computed from the training set.\
  - Per-item mean \uc0\u956 _i computed from training data.\
  - A dense representation (mu_item_full) holds \uc0\u956 _i for all items, with fallback to the global mean if needed.\
- Centered rating matrix:\
  - Build R where R[u, i] = r_ui \uc0\u8722  \u956 _i (rating minus item mean) for training data.\
  - Compute item-item similarity S_raw = R^T R (dense).\
  - Normalize to get S_adjusted = S_raw / (outer(item_norms, item_norms) + epsilon), where item_norms come from the diagonal of S_raw, and epsilon prevents division by zero.\
  - Diagonal of S_adjusted is set to zero (an item is not its own neighbor).\
- Prediction rule (for a given user u and item i):\
  - Consider the top-K item neighbors of i using S_adjusted (K in [5, 10, 20]).\
  - If the user u has rated any neighbor items, compute:\
    - Numerator: sum over neighbor j of s_ij * (r_uj \uc0\u8722  \u956 _j)\
    - Denominator: sum over neighbor j of |s_ij|\
    - Predicted rating: global_mean + Numerator / Denominator\
  - If the user hasn\'92t rated any neighbors, fall back to the global mean.\
  - Clamp predictions to the valid rating range [1, 5].\
- Evaluation:\
  - Evaluates on the validation set for each K in [5, 10, 20].\
  - Metrics computed: RMSE and MAE.\
  - The evaluation uses the same user/item mappings and the training-derived item means for consistency.\
- Outputs:\
  - Saves RMSE and MAE per epoch (per K) to numpy files.\
  - Saves final item means and the adjusted similarity matrix to numpy files.\
  - Keeps an experiment_data structure with metrics and losses.\
\
Key results from your experiment\
- Validation performance by K:\
  - K = 5: RMSE = 1.0181, MAE = 0.8231\
  - K = 10: RMSE = 1.0767, MAE = 0.8660\
  - K = 20: RMSE = 1.1335, MAE = 0.9077\
- These correspond to three evaluation runs (epochs) with K=5, K=10, and K=20, respectively.\
- The results indicate that, on this setup, increasing the number of neighbors (K) did not improve validation performance; RMSE and MAE increased with higher K in this run.\
\
What you can do next ( ideas based on the implementation )\
- Try using real MovieLens data when LensKit is available to assess performance on a standard benchmark.\
- Experiment with different centering/normalization strategies (e.g., user-centric centering, or using residuals r_ui \uc0\u8722  \u956 _item \u8722  \u945 _u where \u945 _u is a user bias).\
- Add regularization or dampening to the similarity scores to reduce noise from sparse data.\
- Compare against other simple baselines (global mean, per-user bias) to gauge the value added by item-based neighbors.\
- Use cross-validation rather than a fixed 80/20 split to get more stable performance estimates.\
\
How to run (summary)\
- The script tries to load MovieLens via LensKit; if unavailable, it builds a synthetic dataset and proceeds.\
